Current camera authority for HLS continuation
=============================================

Heartbeat, playlist, segment and stop requests all require complete session
metadata, the resolved organization scope, a valid camera identity and an
explicit live/recorded flag. Before renewing a heartbeat, decrementing viewers
or returning a file path, each route checks current camera existence, deletion,
site access and the live/playback grant. Refusals do not mutate stream activity
or call the media method.

The API helper now resolves camera identity and site together, using the existing
org_scope_or_platform policy. Only an unscoped super administrator can resolve
platform-wide scope. An org-less scoped key or ordinary principal is refused;
scoped tenant credentials remain restricted to their organization. Platform
sessions compare metadata to that resolved scope, including an unscoped platform
administrator whose user record has an organization assigned.

Missing, deleted or foreign cameras now return 404 consistently at the helper.
Tenant playlist and segment requests ALREADY had a second identity/org/deletion
check inside CameraAccessService before this change: they refused unavailable
cameras with 403. This correction is not evidence of a prior tenant media bypass
on those complete paths. The prior heartbeat and stop routes omitted per-camera
authorization, and the no-org helper returned before current-camera validation.
Incomplete metadata could also skip the playlist/segment per-camera check.

Camera.site_id is NOT NULL in the shipped schema. Identity/site tuple handling
also has a synthetic nullable-site compatibility control; it does not establish
that a migrated database permits cameras without a site. Real qualification must
use valid Site records and actual missing/deleted camera rows.

Playlists and segments send private, no-store cache directives. Playlist polling
also retains no-cache and must-revalidate. This affects new responses only; it
cannot recall media already delivered/buffered or purge previously cached copies.
Existing authentication and cameras.view dependencies remain unchanged.

Qualification requirements
--------------------------
Focused tests must cover all four routes with missing/foreign/deleted cameras,
denied sites and grants, incomplete metadata, scoped credentials, legitimate
platform access and refusal before media methods. Existing Fabric and camera
automation authorization tests retain their assertions; their DB doubles now
return the current identity/site projection. Migrated PostgreSQL qualification
must exercise real CurrentUser, site and CameraAccessService resolution. Synthetic
HLS media methods do not prove FFmpeg, camera hardware or HTTP authentication.
The full frozen source still requires backend, runtime/recovery, browser, image,
source, dependency and packaging qualification before a release claim.

Remaining lifecycle limits
--------------------------
HLS metadata and child-process ownership remain local to the API worker. Multiple
API workers/containers still need a shared ownership and media-serving design;
this change does not fix intermittent cross-worker 404s or duplicate transcoders.
Viewer counts also are not per-viewer leases. A current camera grant authorizes
the existing shared-session operations; it does not identify which viewer joined.

A permission or camera change can race after the final authorization check.
Already-open FileResponse streams and external processes are not transactionally
fenced, and already delivered media cannot be revoked retroactively. Worker-death
recovery, multi-host streaming, recorded playback identity/window deduplication
and durable audit retention require separate qualification.
